WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.298 --> 00:00:02.132
Black Sunday On Sunday, April 14, 1935, a black cloud loomed on the horizon of the Great Plains—the

2
00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:13.940
flat, grassy farmlands in western-central United States. Earlier that day many people picnicked and visited neighbors. The sun

3
00:00:13.941 --> 00:00:22.363
shone and a mild breeze swept the vast fields. But that afternoon an ominous stillness fell over the

4
00:00:22.364 --> 00:00:31.031
plains. The greatest dust storm of the 1930s was about to hit hard. As the storm struck, day

5
00:00:31.032 --> 00:00:39.592
turned into night. Fierce winds churned up the dry soil, completely blocking out the sun. Outdoors, people scrambled

6
00:00:39.593 --> 00:00:46.504
for cover in the swirling darkness while indoors, families huddled together. Many were terrified, certain that the world

7
00:00:46.505 --> 00:00:51.018
was coming to an end. In some places the storm lasted for several hours. When it was over,

8
00:00:51.019 --> 00:00:58.236
dead animals littered the fields. Houses were buried under mounds of dust. Crops were completely destroyed. People

9
00:00:58.237 --> 00:01:05.654
lay dead. The storm on April 14, 1935—later called Black Sunday—was only one of hundreds that pummeled the

10
00:01:05.655 --> 00:01:16.472
area known as the Dust Bowl . A Man-made Disaster During the 1930s parts of the Great Plains

11
00:01:16.473 --> 00:01:18.714
went without rain for almost ten years. Since most of the people living here were farmers and cowboys,

12
00:01:18.715 --> 00:01:24.873
the effects of this drought were devastating. Together with the giant dust storms and intense heat, the lack

13
00:01:24.874 --> 00:01:31.646
of rain ruined crops, killed cattle, destroyed property, and left millions struggling to survive. Though the severe drought

14
00:01:31.647 --> 00:01:42.715
was a major factor in causing the Dust Bowl, many experts believe that it was partly a man-made

15
00:01:42.716 --> 00:01:50.984
disaster. For decades farmers had used the land recklessly because they were unaware of better ways of farming.

16
00:01:50.985 --> 00:01:57.695
They allowed livestock to overgraze. Instead of rotating crops, they planted the same crops in the same fields

17
00:01:57.696 --> 00:02:06.436
every year. They plowed the soil too deeply and planted lots of wheat, a crop that was in

18
00:02:06.437 --> 00:02:15.671
high demand but unable to tolerate drought conditions. Over time the soil became dry and damaged, and America’s

19
00:02:15.672 --> 00:02:23.748
once- lush prairies turned into deserts of dust. Life in the Dust Daily life in the Dust Bowl

20
00:02:23.749 --> 00:02:25.757
was difficult. Crops weren't growing so families lived off things like tumbleweeds and canned food. Despite the drought

21
00:02:25.758 --> 00:02:34.755
many farmers kept plowing the dirt and planting seeds, even though the wind just blew them away. Dust

22
00:02:34.756 --> 00:02:42.506
storms, or "black blizzards," struck frequently, with dry dirt seeping into houses and covering clothes, furniture, and food.

23
00:02:42.507 --> 00:02:54.582
It was in the water and in the air. Avis D. Carlson, a writer, described life this way:

24
00:02:54.583 --> 00:03:03.161
"We live with the dust, eat it, sleep with it." There were also regular insect and pest

25
00:03:03.162 --> 00:03:14.549
invasions. Grasshoppers got into people's clothing and piled up so thickly on train tracks that the trains slipped

26
00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:19.299
right off. Starving jackrabbits in search of food constantly plagued farms. On Sundays people would band together to

27
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:26.308
try to drive them out—sometimes chasing away thousands at once. The dust in the air caused health problems

28
00:03:26.309 --> 00:03:33.290
too. People spat up mud and sometimes became sick. Children were very vulnerable to a disease called dust

29
00:03:33.291 --> 00:03:44.440
pneumonia, a lung infection caused by breathing too much dust. Some families created home remedies to fight its

30
00:03:44.441 --> 00:03:53.609
effects. Unappealing concoctions made with skunk grease, coal oil, turpentine, or lard were either swallowed or rubbed on

31
00:03:53.610 --> 00:04:01.516
the throat. Many children got the disease more than once, and in 1935 the Red Cross declared it

32
00:04:01.517 --> 00:04:03.748
a medical crisis. The Last Man's Club Tired of the dust and hunger, millions of people eventually abandoned

33
00:04:03.749 --> 00:04:10.331
their farms and homes. Many headed west, to places like California, in search of work and a better

34
00:04:10.332 --> 00:04:20.157
life. Churches, businesses, and schools shut their doors too. Most people, however, decided to stay in the Dust

35
00:04:20.158 --> 00:04:28.332
Bowl. When asked why, one woman replied, "Because it is home." A newspaper editor named John McCarty felt

36
00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:38.991
the same way, saying, "One of these days the rains will come again and this land will bloom

37
00:04:38.992 --> 00:04:48.478
like a rose." In 1935, after the Black Sunday storm, McCarty found a way to lift spirits by

38
00:04:48.479 --> 00:04:57.040
forming a group called The Last Man's Club. The club's members each vowed to be the last man

39
00:04:57.041 --> 00:05:02.977
in the Dust Bowl region—they would stay even if everyone else left. McCarty's club inspired many others

40
00:05:02.978 --> 00:05:13.438
to find the courage to stay on their land. Year after year the people waited for rain. Meanwhile

41
00:05:13.439 --> 00:05:21.127
the Dust Bowl region had become so dry and windy that dust was traveling across the country. Twelve

42
00:05:21.128 --> 00:05:23.022
million tons settled in Chicago, and still more drifted as far as New York and Washington DC. Saving

43
00:05:23.023 --> 00:05:31.759
the Soil Americans across the country responded to the crisis in different ways. Ministers and charities offered spiritual

44
00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:40.271
guidance and donated food. Through photographs, songs, and novels, artists captured people’s experiences and raised awareness of the

45
00:05:40.272 --> 00:05:52.171
conditions. Experts on soil conservation helped farmers plant drought-resistant crops and find ways to redirect the wind, encouraging

46
00:05:52.172 --> 00:05:58.825
them to "work with instead of against " the land. In 1936 President Franklin Roosevelt toured the most

47
00:05:58.826 --> 00:06:04.825
devastated areas of the Dust Bowl. In a moving speech, he said it was the whole nation's job

48
00:06:04.826 --> 00:06:10.906
to help families in the plains. The American government set aside two million dollars for soil-conservation projects. The

49
00:06:10.907 --> 00:06:16.410
government also helped by giving relief money and food to families in greatest need. Farmers were encouraged to

50
00:06:16.411 --> 00:06:23.394
follow conservation practices, and they were rewarded a dollar an acre for doing so. Another program enabled the

51
00:06:23.395 --> 00:06:29.230
government to purchase and restore the most severely damaged lands. In 1939 rain finally began to fall, bringing

52
00:06:29.231 --> 00:06:36.382
the decade of dust to an end. A farmer from Kansas remembers, "It was a very emotional time,

53
00:06:36.383 --> 00:06:44.120
when you'd get rain, because it meant so much to you. When the rain came, it meant life

54
00:06:44.121 --> 00:06:49.711
itself. It meant a future." Will the Dust Bowl Return? Many people wonder if storms like Black Sunday,

55
00:06:49.712 --> 00:06:52.121
and a crisis like the Dust Bowl, could ever happen again. Some experts believe they could. They

56
00:06:52.122 --> 00:07:00.532
say that periods of drought and wind are part of the Great Plains climate, and point out that

57
00:07:00.533 --> 00:07:08.533
farmers continue to abuse the land. But others disagree, arguing that today's farmers use better agricultural techniques and

58
00:07:08.534 --> 00:07:16.787
keep their crops watered through irrigation. In addition the government provides support when crops fail or prices fall

59
00:07:16.788 --> 00:07:22.600
too low. Whether another Dust Bowl is created or not, one thing is sure: the farmers of the

60
00:07:22.601 --> 00:07:31.442
Great Plains have a unique relationship with their land. As writer Timothy Egan put it, "It takes a

61
00:07:31.443 --> 00:07:40.008
certain kind of person to make peace with land that has betrayed them, but that is the way

62
00:07:40.009 --> 00:07:46.097
with home." It seems that the wide, beautiful plain will always have a "last man" fighting for it.

